const JOURNEYS = {
  "Anakin": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Slave boy wins the podrace; freed by Qui-Gon Jinn",
     desc:"Nine-year-old slave Anakin Skywalker wins the Boonta Eve Classic on pure instinct, threading Sebulba's sabotage with reflexes beyond his age. Qui-Gon Jinn watches from the sidelines, having bet Anakin's freedom on the outcome. He wins. Midi-chlorian tests confirm an unprecedented Force reading. Qui-Gon frees him but cannot free his mother Shmi. Anakin says goodbye and boards the Naboo starship. Darth Maul intercepts on the outskirts; a brief duel ends in escape. The desert that made him shrinks through the viewport and disappears."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Jedi Council tests him — the Force is strong, but fear is detected",
     desc:"The Jedi Council evaluates Anakin and the Force reading is extraordinary -- the fear reading equally so. Yoda and Mace Windu vote to decline training: too old, too attached, the fear too structural. Qui-Gon pledges to train him anyway. Padme returns to Naboo rather than wait on Senate politics. Palpatine is elected Supreme Chancellor. Anakin departs carrying the Council's refusal, Qui-Gon's promise, and a longing for his mother that no amount of Jedi discipline will ever fully quiet."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Destroys the Droid Control Ship; becomes Obi-Wan's Padawan",
     desc:"Anakin accidentally enters the space battle above Naboo and destroys the Droid Control Ship from inside its own hangar, deactivating every battle droid on the planet. Below, Qui-Gon Jinn is killed by Darth Maul in the generator complex. Obi-Wan bisects Maul and pledges to train Anakin in his dying master's place. Anakin stands at Qui-Gon's funeral pyre and learns, for the first time, that the galaxy gives and takes in the same breath."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Reunited with Padmé as her assigned protector",
     desc:"Ten years later, Anakin is assigned to protect Senator Padme Amidala after an assassination attempt. Their reunion is charged with a decade of unexamined feeling. He tries not to let her see it. He fails. Obi-Wan pursues the assassin, tracing the threat to Kamino. Anakin and Padme depart for Naboo. Palpatine notes the attachment with quiet satisfaction and files it away."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Falls in love at the lake retreat",
     desc:"The Varykino lake retreat makes the Jedi's prohibition on attachment feel abstract. Anakin declares his feelings; Padme deflects. They leave when his visions of Shmi intensify. After Geonosis they return for a secret wedding on the Varykino terrace, witnessed only by R2-D2 and C-3PO. No announcement. The marriage violates the Jedi Code and senatorial convention. They make it anyway with the conviction of people who have decided the rules cannot contain what they feel."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Finds his dying mother; massacres Tusken Raiders",
     desc:"Anakin's nightmares drive him to the Lars farm. Tusken Raiders took Shmi a month ago. He rides into the Jundland Wastes alone, finds her alive, and she dies in his arms. He kills the entire camp and returns to confess to Padme. She holds him rather than condemning him. He stands at the grave and makes a silent promise no one can keep: he will learn to prevent death."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Arena battle; loses his arm to Count Dooku",
     desc:"The execution arena is overrun by Jedi as Mace Windu leads a two-hundred-strong rescue. Anakin pursues Count Dooku alone, his fury both his greatest strength and his worst flaw. Dooku removes his right forearm with one precise stroke. Yoda arrives and forces Dooku's retreat. Anakin wakes in a medical tent with a prosthetic arm and Padme at his side, having made a quiet decision about the future that he shares with no one."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Secret wedding to Padmé",
     desc:"Anakin and Padme return to Varykino after the arena battle to be married in secret. Witnessed only by R2-D2 and C-3PO, they exchange vows on the terrace at sunset. The marriage is forbidden by the Jedi Order and complicated by senatorial convention, but they make it anyway. The sky is quiet over the lake. No one who matters knows yet. The future presses close, carrying everything the code forbids."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Kills Mace Windu; pledges to Palpatine; Order 66 enacted",
     desc:"Palpatine reveals his Sith identity and offers knowledge to prevent Padme's death. Mace Windu's arrest attempt ends with Anakin severing his sword arm. Palpatine's lightning takes Mace from the window. Anakin kneels and accepts the name Darth Vader. Order 66 transmits simultaneously across the galaxy, clone troopers turning on their Jedi commanders. Anakin leads the 501st into the Jedi Temple. Palpatine declares the Galactic Empire. The Republic ends not with a battle but with a choice made in sixty seconds."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Mustafar", beat:"Kills the Separatist Council; duels Obi-Wan; burned alive",
     desc:"Vader executes the Separatist Council, then waits. Padme follows; the reunion fractures instantly as he Force-chokes her, believing Obi-Wan used her against him. The duel across lava platforms ends on a volcanic bank. Obi-Wan takes the high ground. Vader leaps. A single stroke takes both legs and his remaining arm. He burns. Palpatine retrieves what remains. The reconstruction produces the armored figure the galaxy will fear for twenty-three years. Padme dies giving birth to twins she names Luke and Leia."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Rebuilt as Darth Vader in cybernetic armor",
     desc:"What remains of Anakin after Mustafar barely sustains itself: burned, three limbs gone, lungs scoured. Medical droids reconstruct him in stages -- respirator, armor, helmet, the permanent mechanical breath. Darth Vader sits up from the table and says Padme's name. Palpatine tells him she is dead, that in his anger he killed her. It is not entirely true. Vader does not know this. The grief shatters the room in a Force outburst. He stands in the wreckage and breathes, mechanically, rhythmically, permanently."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Force ghost appears redeemed at the Rebel celebration",
     desc:"Luke stands at the edge of the Endor celebration and sees three people only he can see. The Force ghost of Anakin Skywalker -- not the armored monster but a man resolved and at peace -- looks at his son across the distance the living and dead maintain. The face is unhurried; the look contains everything decades of choices made impossible to say. He is not Darth Vader. He has not been Darth Vader since he threw the Emperor into the reactor shaft. He is, and was always, Anakin Skywalker. He smiles."},
  ],
  "Luke": [
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Born; held by his dying mother Padmé",
     desc:"In a sterile asteroid medical bay, Padme Amidala gives birth to twins before dying. Luke is first, placed briefly in his mother's arms before she names him and her strength fails. Obi-Wan and Yoda decide instantly: separate the twins and hide them. Luke goes to Tatooine. His father's stepbrother will raise him as ordinary. The galaxy's most consequential child is swaddled in plain cloth and carried away through asteroid corridors, unnamed to the Empire, unannounced to history."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Delivered to Owen and Beru Lars by Obi-Wan",
     desc:"Obi-Wan Kenobi crosses the Tatooine desert in the blue hour before dawn and sets a sleeping infant on the threshold of the Lars moisture farm. Owen Lars takes him in without ceremony. The decision is made in the same breath: the boy will be raised as Luke Lars, a farmer's son, kept safe from the Empire and from knowledge of his father. The twin suns rise. The galaxy moves on. Nobody knows this happened except the people who need to keep the secret until it's time."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Buys droids; meets Obi-Wan; flees Mos Eisley in the Falcon",
     desc:"Luke Skywalker buys two droids at a Jawa sandcrawler sale and his life changes with the speed of something he didn't see coming. The message in R2-D2 leads him to Obi-Wan Kenobi in the Jundland Wastes. The burned homestead ends the possibility of return. Mos Eisley cantina, Han Solo, the Millennium Falcon. He leaves carrying a lightsaber he doesn't know how to use and a Force sensitivity no one has explained, moving toward a war he just entered without enlisting."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Destroys the Death Star in a single proton torpedo shot",
     desc:"The Death Star plans show a thermal exhaust port two meters wide -- an impossible shot. Luke makes it. He switches off his targeting computer in the trench run and hears Obi-Wan's voice from the place beyond reach: trust the Force. The proton torpedoes enter the shaft, the Death Star detonates in a cascade of light, and one million Imperial crew cease to exist. At the medal ceremony Luke stands in the courtyard as a hero in a war he didn't understand a week ago."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Wampa attack; leads Echo Base until the Imperial assault",
     desc:"A wampa takes him in the ice fields and he hangs upside-down while his body temperature drops. He reaches for his saber with the Force and takes the wampa's arm and survives. Obi-Wan's voice reaches him in the snow, directing him to Dagobah. The Imperial assault on Echo Base: AT-AT walkers advancing across the glacier in the grey morning. He fights, evacuates, and climbs into an X-wing with coordinates for a planet no one has heard of."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Trains with Yoda; leaves early to save his friends",
     desc:"A small green creature by a campfire in a swamp turns out to be the Jedi Master he came to find. Training is physical, strange, and demanding of the stillness Luke doesn't naturally possess. The dark-side cave asks him to face his father and he finds his own face. Yoda warns him: leave now before you're ready and you risk everything. He leaves anyway. He cannot let Han die on a table in Cloud City because a principle says he should stay in a swamp."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Duels Vader; loses hand; learns 'I am your father'",
     desc:"Vader has been waiting for him since he felt Luke in the Force above Hoth. The duel in the carbon-freeze chamber ends at the shaft above the city: Luke's hand gone, hanging over the void. Vader tells him the truth that changes everything: I am your father. Luke lets go of the shaft. The Falcon catches him. He will need time to decide what the truth means, what it requires, what it allows. He doesn't have much."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Returns as a Jedi; rescues Han from Jabba's palace",
     desc:"He walks into Jabba's palace in black and does not take his hood off until he is in the throne room. He negotiates for Han's release; Jabba declines. The gambit involves R2-D2 hiding his lightsaber on the barge and the whole operation unfolding in a sequence of escalating contingencies over the Sarlacc pit. Han is freed. Luke catches his saber, deflects blaster fire with the ease of someone who has practiced this, and leaves a slug crime lord to her fate."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Yoda dies; Obi-Wan reveals Leia is his twin sister",
     desc:"He promised to return and finish his training. He does return, and finds Yoda dying in increments, nine hundred years old, the body becoming notional. Yoda confirms what Vader said: it is true. There is another Skywalker. Yoda passes into the Force and the hut is empty and Luke inherits everything except certainty about what to do with it. Obi-Wan appears from the Force and tells him there is no escape from this confrontation. It is not news. Luke has been trying to figure out how to face his father since Cloud City."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Surrenders to Vader; refuses the Emperor",
     desc:"He turns himself in to Vader at the Endor bunker rather than fight in the battle below. He will not fight his father. On the Death Star he stands before the Emperor and refuses. Vader throws the Emperor into the reactor shaft to save his son: the choice that ends it. Luke carries his father's body to a shuttle, removes the helmet to see his face, and tells him he was right. Vader dies as Anakin Skywalker. Luke burns the armor on the forest moon and watches the smoke rise while fireworks celebrate a victory he knows the full cost of."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Reluctantly trains Rey; becomes one with the Force",
     desc:"He refuses to teach her. Then he teaches her because the Force is happening whether he participates or not. He tells her about Ben Solo, about the moment in the dark room where he ignited his blade above a sleeping student and stopped himself too late. The moment that made Ben Kylo Ren. He cannot undo this. He can watch Rey leave to try anyway. He stands at the island's edge and reaches into the Force and projects himself across the galaxy to the salt flats of Crait, enough time for the Resistance to escape, and lets go."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Force-projects himself to buy the Resistance time to escape",
     desc:"He walks out of the mountain onto the salt flat to face the entire First Order and his nephew. He is not there. He is on Ahch-To, projecting himself across the galaxy through a Force technique that will cost him his life. He faces Kylo Ren's blade and the blade passes through him. He gives the Resistance time to escape through the crystal fox tunnels. On the island he sits in the sunset and becomes one with the Force. He was the last Jedi. He made the time."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Force ghost raises his X-wing from the sea for Rey",
     desc:"Luke Skywalker is gone but not absent. Rey stands on the shore of Ahch-To burning Kylo Ren's TIE fighter and deciding she is done. His voice reaches her from where Force ghosts reach from, warm and without pity. He tells her she has everything she needs. He gives her Leia's lightsaber. Then he raises his X-wing from the sea for her -- the same ship he sank all those years ago, salt-scored and dripping and still flyable. She looks at him. He smiles. She climbs in."},
  ],
  "Leia": [
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Born; held briefly by Padmé before her death",
     desc:"The second twin. She is born moments after Luke, and Padme names her before dying. Bail Organa takes her. He and his wife wanted a daughter; the galaxy has delivered one under circumstances no adoption agency would have predicted. She will be raised on Alderaan as Princess Leia Organa, given every privilege, educated in diplomacy and justice. She will remember nothing of this moment. She will carry its weight for the rest of her life."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Alderaan", beat:"Adopted by Bail and Breha Organa",
     desc:"The infant arrives on Alderaan in Bail Organa's arms and becomes Leia Organa without ceremony. The planet that receives her is a civilization of art, diplomacy, and pacifist conviction -- one of the most cultured worlds in the Core. She will grow up believing Alderaan is her origin. She will grow up believing Bail is her father. Both things will be true in every way that matters. Breha Organa welcomes the child and the story becomes ordinary: a princess in a palace, loved by her parents."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Records the message for Obi-Wan inside R2-D2",
     desc:"The Tantive IV is being boarded and Leia has seconds to act. She records a message and loads it into R2-D2 with the complete Death Star plans, sends the droid toward the escape pods, and is captured trying to reach her own pod. She faces Darth Vader in the corridor with a composure she has been training her entire life. She lies convincingly about the diplomatic mission. He doesn't believe her, but she doesn't break. The cell they put her in is on the Death Star. She will need a plan."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Awards the Medal of Yavin to Luke and Han",
     desc:"She delivers the Death Star plans to the Rebel Alliance and the briefing happens and the attack is planned in hours. She stands on the observation deck and watches the X-wings launch. Han Solo's ship returns from the stars. The explosion of the Death Star is visible from the planet surface. She runs the mission calculations in her head as it burns. Luke Skywalker lands and she puts a medal around his neck. Alderaan is two billion dead and she has not had time to mourn a single one."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Coordinates the Echo Base evacuation",
     desc:"She commands Echo Base through the Imperial assault with the tactical clarity of someone who has been in dangerous rooms since she was nineteen. The order to evacuate comes at the correct moment. She is the last non-combatant out. On the Falcon, the hyperdrive fails, Han Solo is impossible, and the asteroid field is not preferable to an Imperial fleet -- but she chooses it anyway. Control is the only available response to situations where nothing is actually under control."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Captured; witnesses Han frozen in carbonite",
     desc:"Lando Calrissian's betrayal is thorough: the dinner guests are stormtroopers. Han is tortured for Vader's purposes (bait for Luke) and frozen in carbonite alive. She watches it happen. She has been in impossible situations before and survived them through discipline; this one asks her to watch someone she has not admitted she loves be put in a prison he may not wake from. Han hears her say it. He says: I know. Lando's betrayal becomes a partial rescue. It is not enough."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Infiltrates Jabba's palace; strangles him with his own chain",
     desc:"She goes to Jabba's palace disguised as a bounty hunter with a thermal detonator and a plan that half-works. Han is freed from the carbonite but blind. Then the plan unravels: Jabba takes her prisoner and puts her in a costume as a trophy. She waits. When the opening comes she takes the chain that holds her and wraps it around Jabba's neck and holds it until he stops moving. She does not wait to be rescued from this particular situation."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Befriends the Ewoks; learns Luke is her twin brother",
     desc:"She is shot by a stormtrooper in the forest and Wicket finds her. She befriends him with a piece of food and the patience of someone who can always find common ground if she looks for it. Luke tells her the truth about their father: Vader, Anakin, the same man. She absorbs this with the composure she was trained to have. She already knew, on some level. The battle of Endor wins the war. She stands in the firelight as a senator without a senate, a princess without a planet, and she lets herself feel it."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Commands the Resistance; sends Poe on the Jakku mission",
     desc:"She commands the Resistance from D'Qar in the way she has commanded every operation she has ever run -- with precision, presence, and the weight of everyone depending on her distributed across her shoulders invisibly. Han is dead. She felt it. She and Rey find each other in the crowd and hold each other without needing to explain why, two people who lost the same man from different angles. The war is not over. It has not even properly begun. She gives the orders that say so."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Takodana", beat:"Reunites with Han Solo after their long separation",
     desc:"She is at the Resistance base on D'Qar when the signal comes in from Maz Kanata's castle. She goes because Han is there -- they have been separated for years, their grief over Ben pulling them apart rather than together. They stand in the ruins of the castle after the First Order attack and he reaches for her and she lets him. There is no resolution between them -- the wound is too old for one conversation. But she lets him reach for her. It is enough for right now."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Final reunion with Luke through the Force",
     desc:"Kylo Ren's orders his pilots to fire on the bridge. The explosion depressurizes the command deck and Leia is blown into open space. She is frozen, suspended, dying. Then she reaches with the Force -- something she has always had and never formally developed -- and pulls herself back through the vacuum to the airlock. She survives. She wakes in medical. She hears about Luke and knows, the way she always knew things about her brother, exactly what happened and exactly what it cost."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Trains Rey; reaches out to Ben through the Force; dies",
     desc:"She is training Rey and she is also dying -- using the Force at a cost her body cannot sustain indefinitely. She speaks to Ben Solo across the Force, one last time, calling him back to himself. The effort kills her. She dies as her son makes the choice she called him toward: Ben Solo returning. Leia Organa: born in tragedy, senator at fourteen, general in a war that never ended, mother who held on long enough. She becomes one with the Force. The jungle is very quiet."},
  ],
  "Han": [
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Shoots Greedo; hired by Obi-Wan at the Mos Eisley cantina",
     desc:"Mos Eisley cantina, booth in the back, a bounty hunter named Greedo who doesn't leave. Han shoots first. Two strangers offer him a charter: Alderaan, no questions, more money than ten years of freight runs. He takes it for the money. He does not expect to care about the outcome. The old man is strange and the farm kid is strange and the droids are fine. He fires up the Falcon and jumps to hyperspace and tells himself this is just a job."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Returns in the Falcon; saves Luke in the Death Star trench",
     desc:"He takes the money and leaves. He tells himself this is sensible. Then he comes back. The Falcon drops out of hyperspace into the Death Star trench run at the exact moment Vader has Luke's X-wing targeted. He takes out the TIE fighters, clears the path, and Luke makes the shot. He doesn't examine why he came back. He stands in the courtyard with a medal around his neck and Chewie beside him and tries to look like this doesn't mean anything to him."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Rescues Luke with a tauntaun; escapes with Leia",
     desc:"He was leaving -- bounty on his head, debts unpaid, the Falcon packed. Then Luke doesn't come back from patrol and Han rides a tauntaun into the dark and subzero night to find him, because leaving is not something he can do when Luke might be freezing to death somewhere in the dark. He cuts the tauntaun open for warmth and makes a fire from the wreckage of the probe droid and stays with Luke until morning. He is definitely leaving after this."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Frozen in carbonite by Darth Vader; handed to Boba Fett",
     desc:"Lando's hospitality curdles into a Vader ambush over dinner. Han is tortured, not for information -- Vader wants Luke to feel it across the Force -- and then frozen in carbonite alive. Leia says she loves him as the platform descends. He says he knows. It is not bravado: it is the most honest thing he has said in years. The carbonite closes over him. He will be alive inside it, or he won't. Boba Fett takes the block to Jabba. Han Solo disappears from the galaxy."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Thawed out in Jabba's palace; temporarily blind",
     desc:"He comes out of the carbonite blind, disoriented, and immediately in the middle of Leia's rescue operation. He can hear what's happening around him better than he can see it. By the time the skiff battle is over above the Sarlacc pit he can make out shapes and outlines. Luke is a Jedi now -- there's something different in the way he moves. Han is free and alive and has his ship back. That's enough for right now."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Destroys the shield generator with Ewok help",
     desc:"He leads the ground strike team on Endor to destroy the shield generator, and the Ewoks decide his operation needs assistance whether he wants it or not. The battle is chaotic, low-tech, and somehow effective. The shield generator comes down. When the Death Star explodes in the sky above the forest moon he stands in the firelight with Leia and understands that the thing he told himself didn't matter to him has been the thing that mattered most for years."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Takodana", beat:"Takes Rey and Finn to Maz Kanata's castle",
     desc:"Maz Kanata's castle is the kind of place Han Solo ends up when he's navigating by instinct. He brings the two fugitives and the droid and tells himself he'll leave once they're someone else's problem. Then the First Order arrives and there is a firefight in the courtyard and a girl in the forest and the Resistance landing and he doesn't leave. Leia is here. He hasn't seen her in years. They stand in the ruins and don't say any of the things that need to be said."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Emotional reunion with Leia at the Resistance base",
     desc:"He is not there. He died on Starkiller Base. His absence at D'Qar is the shape of the reunion that doesn't happen: the Falcon lands without him, Chewie comes out alone, and the Resistance celebrates a victory at the cost no tally fully accounts for. Leia feels his absence in the Force. Rey feels it in a different way. The man who came back to the trench run at Yavin 4 when he didn't have to came back every time after that, until the one time he couldn't."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Starkiller Base", beat:"Confronts Kylo Ren on the bridge — killed by his son",
     desc:"He finds his son on the oscillator bridge and calls to him: Ben. He has rehearsed this conversation for years in the version where it ends differently. Ben Solo is in there, Leia said. Kylo Ren removes his helmet and Han sees his son's face and reaches toward it one last time. His son takes his lightsaber and drives it through his chest. Han touches Ben's face. He falls. The oscillator goes down. The planet tears itself apart. Han Solo's last act is to reach toward his son."},
  ],
  "Chewbacca": [
    {ep:"III", planet:"Kashyyyk", beat:"Leads Wookiee warriors; helps Yoda escape Order 66",
     desc:"The Battle of Kashyyyk: Republic forces and Wookiee warriors defend the treetop cities against Separatist droids. Yoda commands alongside Chewbacca, who fights with the ferocity of someone defending his home. Order 66 activates. Clone Commander Gree attempts to execute Yoda; Yoda kills him first. Chewbacca carries Yoda to an escape pod through root passages below the city. He will not see Yoda again for over two decades. He files this in the section of his memory reserved for the things he was glad to do."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Mos Eisley cantina; boards the Falcon with Ben and Luke",
     desc:"Han Solo's first mate, co-pilot, and the person who keeps the Millennium Falcon operational through creative application of Wookiee engineering. He is in the cantina when the strangers hire Han, in the cockpit for the Kessel Run that earned them the job, and beside Han at every point afterward. He expresses opinion on every decision through vocalization. Han translates approximately. The actual content of Chewbacca's commentary is usually more accurate than the translation."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Co-pilots the Falcon during the Battle of Yavin",
     desc:"He sits beside Han in the Falcon cockpit for the Death Star trench run and co-pilots the return from the asteroid field and is present for everything the Falcon does in the Battle of Yavin. He does not receive a medal at the ceremony. This is a protocol oversight that becomes, over time, one of the more persistent footnotes in galactic history. He was there. He roars at Luke in the landing bay with the approval of someone who has assessed the situation and found it adequate."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Evacuates Echo Base while repairing the Falcon's hyperdrive",
     desc:"He is in the Falcon's engine compartment for most of the Battle of Hoth, addressing the hyperdrive motivator with the urgency of a co-pilot who understands that the ship's survival depends on the repair and the battle's timeline doesn't care about the difficulty. The hyperdrive doesn't engage. He keeps working. The Falcon escapes the Imperial fleet through the asteroid field at speeds that would worry a more cautious Wookiee. He is not a more cautious Wookiee."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Carries C-3PO's pieces; witnesses Han's carbon-freezing",
     desc:"He reassembles C-3PO from pieces collected from the Bespin junk corridors, because C-3PO is a member of the crew and members of the crew get reassembled. When Han is frozen in carbonite Chewbacca's response is immediate and violent; it takes Leia to stop him from destroying the room. He carries C-3PO's parts on his back through the Bespin escape because that's what you do with crew members. The half-assembled droid talks the entire time."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Disguised as prisoner to infiltrate Jabba's palace",
     desc:"He is captured alongside Han as part of Leia's rescue operation and brought before Jabba in binders. He sits in Jabba's dungeon with Han and waits for the plan to develop, which is his general approach to operations designed by people shorter than him. The battle on the Sail Barge gives him the opportunity he prefers: direct engagement. He fights. Han is free. The Sarlacc gets a Hutt."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Commandeers an AT-ST with the Ewoks",
     desc:"He commandeers an AT-ST during the Battle of Endor by the simple method of climbing on it, ripping the hatch open, and removing the operators. He pilots it into the Imperial installation gate while Ewoks engage the ground forces. The shield generator comes down. He roars at the explosion in the sky above the forest with the specific volume of a Wookiee who has been fighting this war since the Clone Wars and has just watched it end."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Takodana", beat:"Wounds Kylo Ren with the bowcaster",
     desc:"He is in the Falcon with Han when the two fugitives and the droid are located by the ship's tracking system. He adjusts to the new passengers with the equanimity of a first mate who has seen Han Solo's cargo manifest change unexpectedly before. At Maz Kanata's castle he fights the First Order assault in the courtyard and is wounded by a bowcaster bolt that he applies to enemy troops with immediate effect for the rest of the battle."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Starkiller Base", beat:"Plants charges; devastated as Kylo kills Han",
     desc:"He watches Han Solo die on the oscillator bridge and the grief is immediate and absolute. He fires on Kylo Ren -- the son, the man who killed his partner of fifty years -- hits him in the side. Then he does what Han would have needed him to do: sets the charges, completes the mission, gets Rey and Finn to the Falcon. He flies them out as the planet tears apart. Han Solo has been beside him in that cockpit for five decades. The seat is empty. He flies."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Befriends the Porgs; bonds with Luke's island",
     desc:"Chewbacca lands the Falcon on Ahch-To while Rey finds Luke. The island's porg population immediately investigates the Millennium Falcon with the thoroughness of creatures that have never encountered anything like it. They get into everything. Chewbacca removes them repeatedly. They return. He makes camp. He reconnects with Luke -- his old friend, older now, with the specific quiet of someone who has decided something and is still deciding if they decided right. Chewbacca notes this without needing to vocalize it."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Pilots the Falcon to evacuate the surviving Resistance fighters",
     desc:"He co-pilots the Falcon in the desperate Crait diversion, threading the crystal fox canyon passes at speeds the ship is not technically rated for. The goal is to distract the First Order long enough for the survivors to escape through the tunnel. He does this without commentary beyond necessary operational vocalization. The Resistance escapes. Luke is gone -- he felt it, the way you feel the Force move when someone you have known a long time passes through it and disappears."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Pasaana", beat:"Captured by First Order stormtroopers",
     desc:"The escape from the quicksand below the Pasaana desert goes wrong at the final moment: stormtroopers are already waiting above. Chewbacca is separated from the group and taken by First Order troops to a transport ship. He is alive but in captivity, which is a circumstance he has been in before and has not found to improve with repetition. The Resistance believes briefly that he died in an explosion. He did not. He waits. He has always been good at waiting when there is no better option."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Leads the Falcon into the Battle of Exegol",
     desc:"He is in the Falcon when the fleet of a thousand ships arrives above Exegol to answer Lando's call. He co-pilots through the Final Order fleet with the focused ferocity of a Wookiee who has been fighting the same war in different uniforms since the Clone Wars. The Final Order fleet is destroyed. The galaxy is free. He receives a medal -- a detail that matters more to some people than others, but which the official record will note this time."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Receives Han's medal from Maz Kanata at the celebration",
     desc:"He has been flying with Leia's Resistance since the Starkiller disaster. He is on Ajan Kloss for the briefing, for the planning, for the missions that follow. When Leia dies he feels it from wherever the Falcon is and it is the third great loss of his long life: Kashyyyk, Han, Leia. He carries them forward because Wookiees carry what they love and do not put it down. He flies into the Battle of Exegol with the fleet that answers the call, and this time there are enough of them."},
  ],
  "Obi-Wan": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Trade Federation negotiations; escapes with Queen Amidala",
     desc:"Qui-Gon's apprentice, present for everything his master does and not always consulted about it. He follows Qui-Gon into the Trade Federation trap, out of it, through the planet's core, to Theed, and onto Queen Amidala's ship. He is competent and loyal and has learned to trust his master's instincts even when they outrun the Council's approval. He watches Qui-Gon find the boy on Tatooine and sees something he will spend years trying to understand: a certainty that seems to bypass normal Jedi caution entirely."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Waits aboard the ship while Qui-Gon finds Anakin",
     desc:"He is in the Falcon -- no, wrong film. He watches Qui-Gon find the boy in Anakin's workshop and assess him with the practiced eye of a Jedi attuned to the living Force. He sees the midi-chlorian test and hears the number and understands why Qui-Gon can't walk away. He also sees a nine-year-old child who says goodbye to his mother with the full weight of someone who understands he may not come back. He files this carefully. He will return to it often."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Reports the Sith sighting to the Jedi Council",
     desc:"He stands with Qui-Gon before the Jedi Council and watches his master argue for training a boy the Council has declined. He does not contradict his master publicly. He has reservations -- the attachment is visible, the fear is visible -- but he trusts Qui-Gon's judgment more than his own doubt. When the Council finally agrees after Qui-Gon's death, Obi-Wan inherits both the promise and the responsibility for whatever comes of it."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Kills Darth Maul; knighted; pledges to train Anakin",
     desc:"Darth Maul kills Qui-Gon in the generator core and Obi-Wan kills Maul in the same sequence, bisecting him into the pit. Then he kneels with his master's dying body. Qui-Gon extracts one promise: train the boy. Obi-Wan agrees. He stands up from this moment as Qui-Gon's promise-keeper, the boy's master, and the man who has just killed a Sith alone at Padawan rank. He is twenty-five. He will not stop carrying this moment until he stands on a desert planet thirty-two years later and lets Darth Vader's blade pass through him."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Investigates the assassination attempt on Padmé",
     desc:"Ten years later he is a Jedi Knight and Anakin is his Padawan and the dynamic between them is warm, complicated, and occasionally maddening. The assassination attempt on Padme Amidala splits them: Anakin goes with Padme to Naboo, Obi-Wan follows the dart to Kamino and finds a clone army that nobody ordered. The trail leads to Geonosis. He sends the coordinates before the trap closes. He is captured. He waits."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Kamino", beat:"Discovers the secret clone army; fights Jango Fett",
     desc:"The dart leads him to Kamino -- an ocean world not in the Jedi Archives, its entry erased. The Kaminoans greet him as expected; they have been waiting for a Jedi to inspect the army commissioned in Master Sifo-Dyas's name. He tours the facility with the composure of a Jedi who is filing everything away rather than reacting. Ten million clones in production. He fights Jango Fett on the landing platform in a rainstorm and loses him, but gets a blood sample. He sends coordinates to the Council and is captured on Geonosis shortly after."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Captured in the arena; duels Count Dooku",
     desc:"He is chained in the Geonosian arena when the Jedi rescue arrives. He fights in the battle, pursues Dooku, duels him, and loses: Dooku takes his arm and leg in quick sequence. Yoda arrives in time to prevent him from losing his life. He wakes in a medical tent having confirmed what he suspected: Dooku is not acting alone, the war is constructed, and the Republic is walking into something much larger than a Separatist dispute."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Battle of Coruscant; rescues Palpatine from Grievous",
     desc:"The Battle of Coruscant opens with Obi-Wan and Anakin flying into a trap to rescue Chancellor Palpatine from General Grievous's ship. They retrieve Palpatine, execute Count Dooku, and crash the flaming starship into a Coruscant landing platform. Obi-Wan senses something wrong in Anakin that he cannot name. The Council asks him to spy on Palpatine using Anakin. He is uncomfortable with this. He does it anyway. He is wrong about more than he knows."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Utapau", beat:"Kills General Grievous on the sinkhole world",
     desc:"General Grievous is on Utapau. Obi-Wan descends into the sinkhole city on a varactyl and kills Grievous with a blaster -- uncivilized, but effective. Order 66 activates mid-battle. His clone escort turns on him and he falls into the water below the city. He escapes on Grievous's starfighter and contacts Bail Organa. He learns what Order 66 has done. He returns to Coruscant to find out the full scope of it, knowing already that the scope is catastrophic."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Mustafar", beat:"Duels and defeats Anakin on the lava banks",
     desc:"He goes to Mustafar because someone must. Padme takes him without knowing why. Anakin sees them together and it confirms the story Palpatine told him. The duel follows: lava platforms, catwalks, the full length of their relationship's arc expressed in blade work. Obi-Wan takes the high ground. Anakin leaps. One stroke. He leaves Anakin burning on the volcanic bank because he cannot kill him and cannot save him and cannot stay. He takes Padme to Polis Massa. He says goodbye to Anakin Skywalker on the lava shore and walks away."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Present at the birth of Luke and Leia",
     desc:"He brings Padme to the asteroid facility and she delivers twins before dying. He names them with Yoda and Bail: separate them, hide them. He volunteers for Tatooine because Anakin came from there and the Force might want something to close. He delivers Luke to Owen and Beru Lars at dawn. He tells Owen he will be nearby if needed. Owen doesn't want him nearby. He takes a position in the Jundland Wastes and begins the longest wait of his life."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Delivers Luke to the Lars homestead; begins exile",
     desc:"He crosses the Jundland Wastes at dawn and leaves Luke Skywalker on the Lars homestead threshold with the care of someone who understands that this delivery is the most important thing he will do in the next nineteen years. He tells Owen Lars he will be nearby. Owen doesn't want him nearby. He finds a stone dwelling in the wastes, settles, and begins. He will watch the boy grow up at a respectful distance. He will practice patience that does not come naturally. He has something to wait for. He waits."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Reveals Jedi past to Luke; joins the mission",
     desc:"He has been watching the boy for nineteen years from a distance that respects Owen Lars's wishes. When R2-D2 plays the message and Luke finds him in the Wastes, the timing feels like the Force rather than coincidence. He tells Luke about his father -- the version he has decided to tell, which is true from a certain point of view. He gives Luke the lightsaber. He goes with them to Mos Eisley. He is doing what Qui-Gon asked him to do thirty-two years ago."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Force-vision tells Luke to seek Yoda",
     desc:"He appears to Luke on Dagobah as a Force ghost, blue and luminous, and confirms the direction: train with Yoda. He watches the training and appears again when Luke prepares to leave for Bespin, warning him that Vader is a lure and that incomplete training costs more than it protects. Luke goes anyway. Obi-Wan watches him go and says nothing. He understands the limits of what he can require of the living. He said the same things to Anakin once, differently, and was wrong about what they would cost."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Force ghost reveals that Leia is Luke's twin sister",
     desc:"He appears to Luke after Yoda's death and is asked to account for himself. He tries. The certain-point-of-view explanation is as honest as he can make it about something he's been carrying for twenty years. He tells Luke the truth a boy could survive hearing, not the full truth. Obi-Wan explains rather than defends. He tells Luke what he hasn't told him yet: there is another Skywalker. He watches Luke work through the recognition. He releases him to what comes next and disappears back into the Force."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Force ghost appears at the Rebel celebration",
     desc:"He stands in the Endor firelight beside Yoda and Anakin Skywalker and watches Luke see him. The expression on Luke's face is the thing he wondered about for thirty years: what it would look like when it had gone right. It went right. The variable he could never model was Anakin himself -- what remained underneath everything done to him and done by him. The answer was always yes. It required a son willing to go alone and refuse to give up. The fire burns. Obi-Wan smiles."},
  ],
  "Yoda": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Evaluates Anakin; senses great fear in him",
     desc:"Nine hundred years of living has not made Yoda comfortable with certainty. He feels the boy from Tatooine's extraordinary Force potential and the fear woven through his attachment to people he loves. He votes to decline training, over Qui-Gon's objection. The kind of fear this boy carries does not resolve with reassurance. He is overruled in the end by a dying man's last request. He notes this, and what it means, says nothing further, and watches the boy leave."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Attends Qui-Gon Jinn's funeral pyre",
     desc:"Qui-Gon Jinn followed the living Force rather than the Council's models, and was right often enough that his heterodoxy was tolerated. Yoda stands at his funeral pyre and thinks about what it means that the one Jedi most capable of guiding this particular student has been taken in the moment before the guidance began. He watches the fire until it is done and carries the weight home without arriving at a conclusion."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Jedi Council deliberates over the Separatist threat",
     desc:"The Separatist movement has been building for years and the Jedi Council watches with the discomfort of an institution that can see a storm but has not agreed on what to do. Dooku, Yoda's own former Padawan, is leading it. Yoda senses the architecture: an elegant, patient construction of a trap whose walls are made of legitimate crises. He participates with the patience of someone who has attended nine hundred years of meetings and knows this one will not produce clarity."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Leads the Jedi rescue; duels Count Dooku with full power",
     desc:"He arrives in a gunship and finds Dooku waiting. The duel is brief and absolute: Yoda deflecting lightning with a raised hand, a green blade against a red one. He does not defeat Dooku. Dooku drops a structure on Obi-Wan and Anakin and flees while Yoda diverts to stop the crash -- a calculation Dooku made correctly: Yoda will always choose the lives he can save over the enemy he can catch. A limitation and a definition simultaneously."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Jedi Council; fights Palpatine in the Senate chamber; flees",
     desc:"He knows before he enters the Chancellor's office. He kills the guards and faces Darth Sidious in the Senate chamber with the full power of nine hundred years of Force mastery. The fight shatters the rotunda. He does not win. He does not lose. He escapes -- the only outcome that preserves the possibility of something better. He retreats into exile on a planet that does not appear on any map."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Kashyyyk", beat:"Leads the Battle of Kashyyyk; survives Order 66",
     desc:"Yoda fights alongside Wookiee warriors in the wroshyr tree canopy. Order 66 activates and Yoda feels it before he sees it: the deaths of hundreds of Jedi across the galaxy in the same moment. Clone Commander Gree raises his weapon from behind. Yoda's blade is faster. Chewbacca guides him to an escape pod through root passages only Wookiees know. He sits alone in the pod extending his senses across a galaxy gone silent where the Jedi were."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Witnesses Luke and Leia's birth; communes with Qui-Gon's spirit",
     desc:"Two children are born. A woman dies. Yoda sits with it all: nine hundred years of practice with loss that produces not numbness but the capacity to hold grief without being crushed. He tells Obi-Wan what to do with the twins, tells Bail what is required. Then he reaches for Qui-Gon, and Qui-Gon reaches back. There is a way to persist after death. He will learn it. The exile begins to look like preparation rather than defeat."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Begins 19 years of exile",
     desc:"Yoda chose Dagobah for practical and personal reasons: the planet's dense Force-signature masks his own; it embodies the lesson the galaxy keeps failing to learn about underestimating small things. The swamp receives him without ceremony. He communes with Qui-Gon's spirit and waits. He does not experience the waiting as defeat, because defeat requires expecting a different outcome, and his expectations have been calibrated to what the Force requires rather than what he prefers."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Reluctantly trains Luke Skywalker in the ways of the Force",
     desc:"The boy arrives crashing through the canopy and Yoda watches from cover, assessing his patience by pretending to be an eccentric swamp creature before revealing himself. Training: lifting stones, the dark-side cave, learning to listen rather than impose. When Luke leaves early, Yoda warns that incomplete training produces incomplete Jedi, and that choosing friends over completion is how the dark side recruits. He watches Luke go. He knows what this choice will cost. He knows what it will also teach."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Dies at 900 years old; becomes one with the Force",
     desc:"He is dying in the way everything very old dies: incrementally, the body becoming more notional than structural. Nine hundred years old. He addresses what needs addressing: there is another Skywalker. He is tired in the way nine hundred years of anything produces, and the tiredness feels correct, the last page of a long book. He becomes one with the Force without drama, which is how he has always preferred most things. The hut is empty. Luke inherits everything."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Force ghost appears beside Obi-Wan and Anakin",
     desc:"He appears beside Obi-Wan and Anakin in the Endor firelight because presence is itself a kind of meaning. Small and luminous, entirely himself. He looks at Luke from across the fire and allows himself the satisfaction of a very long bet that has paid off. He was not certain. After nine hundred years he has learned to hold possibilities rather than certainties, to maintain hope as a practice rather than a prediction. The bet paid off."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Force ghost burns the sacred Jedi texts; counsels Luke",
     desc:"He appears in the rain because it suits what he came to do. Luke stands outside the sacred tree unable to burn it. So Yoda calls down lightning and does it for him, cheerfully, because he has thought about this and is enjoying being correct slightly more than is dignified. The texts are in Rey's bag already; Yoda knows this, Luke does not, and this is funny. He sits with Luke in the rain and tells him: failure is the greatest teacher. He knows what he is talking about."},
  ],
  "Qui-Gon": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Escapes the Trade Federation blockade with Queen Amidala",
     desc:"Qui-Gon Jinn practices the living Force -- the immediate pulse of what is actually happening rather than what the Council's models predict. He arrives on Naboo for a trade negotiation that turns into an invasion within minutes. He escapes with Obi-Wan, extracts Queen Amidala and her court, and guides them to a ship through a planet core passage that Jar Jar Binks knows. He operates with the combination of planning and absolute confidence that makes him effective when the original plan has stopped applying."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Discovers Anakin; wins his freedom in a podrace bet",
     desc:"The hyperdrive is damaged and they need parts that require currency they don't have. Qui-Gon barters his way into an arrangement with Watto and notices a nine-year-old slave with Force sensitivity that exceeds anything in living Jedi memory. He makes the bet, wins the podrace, and frees the boy. He cannot free the mother. He takes what the Force has delivered and boards the ship with Anakin, carrying a certainty about this child that he knows the Council will not share."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Reports the Sith sighting; presents Anakin to the Council",
     desc:"He presents Anakin to the Jedi Council with the conviction of a man who has already decided what needs to happen. The Council tests the boy, finds the midi-chlorian count unprecedented, and votes no: too old, too much fear and attachment. Qui-Gon argues. He loses the vote and pledges to train the boy himself. He is not asking permission. He will train Anakin whether or not the Council approves, and everyone in the room understands this."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Killed by Darth Maul; cremated; Obi-Wan pledges to train Anakin",
     desc:"He faces Darth Maul in the Theed generator complex alone after the force field separates him from Obi-Wan. He fights the full length of the facility and it is not enough. Maul drives his blade through Qui-Gon's torso at the edge of the shaft. He falls. Obi-Wan screams from behind the force field. When the field drops, Obi-Wan kills Maul and comes to kneel with him. He extracts one promise. Obi-Wan gives it. He lets go."},
  ],
  "Mace": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Jedi Council refuses to train Anakin initially",
     desc:"Mace Windu sits at the center of a Jedi Council that is beginning to feel the contours of something wrong without being able to name it. A Sith Lord has appeared -- the first in a thousand years -- and a boy has appeared with an unprecedented Force reading and both things happened on the same planet on the same day. He votes to decline training Anakin. He is right about the danger and wrong about the solution, a combination that will define his legacy."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Council debates the growing Separatist threat",
     desc:"He speaks at Jedi Council sessions about the Separatist crisis, about Dooku, about the dark side growing in ways the Council can sense but not locate. He approves the investigation of the clone army and the Kamino connection and sends Obi-Wan. He is a leader of an institution navigating a crisis it helped create by not acting sooner, moving with the gravity of an organization that has been certain of its own judgment for too long."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Leads the Jedi strike team; beheads Jango Fett",
     desc:"He leads two hundred Jedi into the Geonosian execution arena with a blade in his hand and does not announce himself -- he simply appears at Jedi Master Kenobi's execution and the rescue begins. He kills, conserves, commands, and by the end of the battle has overseen the opening engagement of a war that will last three years and end with the Jedi Order's destruction. He does not know this yet. He is winning."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Arrests Palpatine; overpowered by Force lightning and Anakin's betrayal",
     desc:"He goes to arrest Palpatine with three Masters and comes back alone, disarmed. He has Palpatine's blade hand at the window and is about to end it when Anakin intervenes. Palpatine's lightning takes him from the window. Mace Windu falls from the Senate tower over Coruscant. He was right about Palpatine for years. He was wrong about Anakin, about what the boy would do at the critical moment. The Order dies with him. He knew what was at stake. He fell anyway."},
  ],
  "Palpatine": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Senator Palpatine orchestrates his rise to Supreme Chancellor",
     desc:"Senator Palpatine from Naboo is elected Supreme Chancellor of the Galactic Republic through the mechanism of a manufactured crisis that he arranged. He expresses appropriate gravity about the responsibility. He mentions young Anakin to Qui-Gon Jinn with the warmth of someone who has filed the boy away as a future asset. He has been Darth Sidious for decades. No one knows. The Senate votes. The Senate has just handed him everything he needs."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Greets returning Queen Amidala; attends Qui-Gon's funeral",
     desc:"He greets Queen Amidala's return to Naboo with the warmth of a fellow Naboo citizen who wanted her victory even more than he appeared to. The liberation plays out as he expected. Qui-Gon Jinn's funeral follows -- a ceremony for a Jedi who stood between him and the boy one time. Now the boy will be trained by a young Padawan rather than a master. He files this as a variable to manage later and stands in the appropriate position at the procession with appropriate gravity. He is very good at appropriate gravity."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Granted emergency powers; deploys the clone army",
     desc:"He plays the role of the embattled Chancellor with the patience of an architect who can see every wall he has built and every wall that will fall. He watches Anakin -- now his Padawan protege -- with the specific attention of someone cultivating an investment. The Separatist crisis is his design. The Senate will vote him emergency powers in weeks. He expresses concern about the Jedi's ability to protect him and the Republic. He is not concerned."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Enacts Order 66; declares the Galactic Empire",
     desc:"He tells Anakin the tragedy of Darth Plagueis at the opera: a Sith Lord who learned to prevent death and taught his apprentice everything he knew. Then his apprentice killed him in his sleep. He lets the implication sit. When Anakin is ready -- desperate enough about Padme's nightmares -- he reveals himself. Anakin serves him. Order 66 goes out. The Jedi are purged. He declares the Galactic Empire before the full Senate and they applaud."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Mustafar", beat:"Personally retrieves the burned Vader from the lava bank",
     desc:"He sends Vader to Mustafar to execute the Separatist Council. He does not go himself. When Vader is defeated and burning he retrieves what remains -- the commitment has been made, the investment cannot be abandoned mid-way. He has Vader rebuilt in the reconstruction theater on Coruscant. He stands over the table as the armor closes. He tells Vader that Padme is dead. He watches the room shatter in a Force outburst. He has made what he needed."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Tries to turn Luke to the dark side; killed by Vader's sacrifice",
     desc:"He receives Luke Skywalker in the throne room of the second Death Star and invites him to watch the battle below: a demonstration that the trap is complete. He wants Luke angry. Anger leads to strikes. A strike leads to succession. He has calculated this with thirty years of patience. What he has not calculated is the man in the armor standing behind his throne. Vader lifts him and throws him into the reactor shaft while his own lightning fires. He falls. The Emperor of the galaxy, dead by the one person he believed he owned."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Revealed alive as Sith Eternal; destroyed by Rey's reflected lightning",
     desc:"He has been dead for thirty years and has not stopped. On Exegol, deep in the Sith citadel, something has been sustained through ancient Sith alchemy: not resurrection, but persistence. He has built the Final Order fleet in secret, rebuilt himself in failing bodies, and waited for a Palpatine heir to claim the throne or a Skywalker to strike him down and be used as a vessel. Rey is a Palpatine. He has all the pieces. He has miscounted again."},
  ],
  "R2-D2": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Repairs the Royal Starship's shields under fire",
     desc:"An astromech droid aboard Queen Amidala's flagship scrambles to repair the deflector shield under Naboo blockade fire. Every other droid on the hull is destroyed. R2-D2 completes the repair. The ship escapes. The Queen's handmaiden comes to the droid bay and thanks him. He acknowledges this with the specific combination of bleeps that expresses satisfaction in a completed task."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Meets C-3PO for the first time in Anakin's workshop",
     desc:"Anakin Skywalker's workshop is organized chaos presided over by an incomplete protocol droid whose wiring shows through gaps in his plating. C-3PO introduces himself with the formality of a droid who takes his own incomplete existence very seriously. R2-D2 responds with the direct practicality of a utility droid that has already assessed the situation and found the tall golden droid interesting in ways exceeding immediate utility. They are very different kinds of machine. They understand each other immediately."},
    {ep:"I", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Travels with the Royal entourage to the Senate",
     desc:"R2-D2 travels with Queen Amidala's entourage through the Senate district, observing the planet with the data-collection orientation of a droid designed to notice things. He notes the political anxiety beneath formal courtesy, the way Senator Palpatine speaks about the Queen's situation with the warmth of a man who has already decided how it will resolve. He notes everything with the thoroughness of a system that keeps records by design, witnessing events historically significant before he has the context to understand why."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Accompanies Anakin and Padmé on protection detail",
     desc:"R2-D2 maintains his post with the patience of a droid that processes time differently from organics, monitoring building systems and ambient sensor data. He accompanies Anakin and Padme through the protection assignment with steady reliability, understanding that his primary function requires constant data collection rather than reactive response. Anakin has grown considerably since Tatooine: more confident, more frustrated with the constraints of his situation. R2-D2 files both observations in the section reserved for human behavioral prediction."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Witnesses Anakin and Padmé's secret wedding ceremony",
     desc:"The Naboo lake house at sunset creates the atmospheric conditions that organics respond to with increased emotional intensity. R2-D2 observes the ceremony with the thoroughness he applies to everything: the holy man, the words exchanged, the quality of stillness that descends over Anakin when he takes Padme's hand. His memory banks record all of it without editorial function. He will carry this memory through the next twenty-two years, through dormancy and reactivation, never asked about it and never volunteering it."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Rescues Padmé's unconscious body in the arena",
     desc:"The Geonosian arena is sensor-overwhelming noise and R2-D2 parses it. He identifies Padme Amidala's location -- she has fallen from a reek -- and navigates toward her through the ongoing battle with the determination of a droid that has identified a priority objective. He reaches her. He has no weapons capable of engaging battle droids directly, so he navigates around them, using chaos as cover. Find what you can do and do it completely."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Participates in the Battle of Coruscant space fight",
     desc:"The space above Coruscant is full of fire and R2-D2 is in it, in his socket behind Anakin's cockpit. He manages the starfighter's systems while Anakin and Obi-Wan coordinate above him. He zaps two buzz droids off Obi-Wan's hull with his electro-shock system, having calculated their likely trajectory and the available countermeasures, executing the solution without waiting for instruction because waiting for instruction in situations requiring immediate response is a failure mode he has identified and corrected."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Mustafar", beat:"Carries Anakin's starfighter to Mustafar",
     desc:"R2-D2 monitors the starfighter's systems as Anakin descends through Mustafar's hostile atmosphere. He lands and waits. He observes Padme's ship arriving. He observes the meeting that follows. He cannot intervene in what happens next; he is an astromech droid, and the crisis is beyond his systems to address. He stays with the ship. He monitors. He carries it all away when the ship lifts."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Accompanies Obi-Wan; present at twins' birth",
     desc:"R2-D2 accompanies Obi-Wan through the Polis Massa facility with the quiet presence of a droid that understands when to be present without requiring acknowledgment. He witnesses the births and records the names -- Luke and Leia -- in the section of his memory dedicated to the people he is assigned to serve. He will hold these memories through a dormancy spanning the entire reign of the Empire, and the names of the children will remain unshared until the moment the Force requires them to be useful."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Carries Leia's message; escapes in an escape pod",
     desc:"The mission is to get Leia's message to Obi-Wan Kenobi, and the Devastator is boarding the Tantive IV. R2-D2 heads for the escape pods. C-3PO is already there, protesting on the grounds that there is no planetary system to escape toward. R2-D2 overrides the objection and fires the pod toward Tatooine below, which he has been to before; he does not share this with C-3PO because C-3PO's response would be distracting. He begins processing the route to Obi-Wan Kenobi."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Delivers the Death Star plans; damaged in the trench run",
     desc:"The Death Star plans in his memory banks represent the most strategically important data in the Rebel Alliance's possession. He delivers them and the battle planning uses his data. He is in Luke's X-wing for the trench run, monitoring the fighter's systems. A TIE fighter's cannon hit damages him during the run and he goes dark. He is repaired on Yavin 4 and comes back online to find the battle won and Luke looking at him with the expression of someone relieved that something they care about has survived."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Joins Luke's snowspeeder squadron in the Battle of Hoth",
     desc:"R2-D2 is in Luke's snowspeeder for the Battle of Hoth, monitoring the speeder's systems. The AT-AT walkers are large targets with significant armor ratings; his threat assessment runs countermeasure calculations continuously. Luke's tow-cable method works once and he records the success before the battle deteriorates. He evacuates with Luke toward the coordinates of a swamp planet navigated toward with a certainty his sensors cannot fully account for."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Lands in the swamp; encounters Yoda with Luke",
     desc:"Dagobah's atmospheric density makes precise touchdown navigation difficult. He lands the X-wing in a swamp and is immediately nose-down in it. He gets out. He is immediately accosted by a swamp slug. He discharges a mild electro-shock. The slug withdraws. A small green creature appears and examines him with the thoroughness of someone evaluating droids for significantly longer than the droid has been operational. He does not know at this point who the small green creature is. He knows shortly afterward."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Repairs the Falcon's hyperdrive — too late to help Han",
     desc:"The Millennium Falcon's hyperdrive motivator has a fault he identified on the first pass and has been correcting through successive modifications. He is in the hyperdrive access corridor making the repair when Lando's maintenance droid undoes the work he has just completed. He gets the hyperdrive working in time to escape Cloud City. He does not get it working in time to stop what happens to Han. He will run this sequence through his memory many times."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Serves drinks at Jabba's palace as part of the rescue plan",
     desc:"R2-D2 is loaded with drinks in a modified serving configuration and wheeled into Jabba's court. He observes the environment: layout, personnel, guard positions, the location of the relevant pieces of the plan. He carries Luke's lightsaber in his dome storage, waiting for the signal with the patience of a droid that processes time differently from organics. When the signal comes he extends the saber launcher and Luke catches it over the Sarlacc pit. The plan moves to its next phase."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Dagobah", beat:"Waits outside Yoda's hut during Luke's final visit",
     desc:"He waits outside Yoda's hut with the quality of presence he has learned to bring to significant moments. He monitors ambient Force signatures with the limited sensitivity his systems can register -- a detectable variation in electromagnetic readings that correlates with significant Force events. He detects something significant. When Luke comes out his expression carries grief combined with resolve, a combination R2-D2 has seen before in contexts that preceded significant action."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Opens the Imperial bunker door for the strike team",
     desc:"The Imperial bunker door is a Type-7 security installation with a standard Galactic Empire access system that R2-D2 has the decryption protocols for. He wheels up to the control panel while the battle rages in the forest. He plugs in. The decryption runs. An Imperial alert fires through the system and his dome lights up, his dome cover blows off, he sprays sparks. He completes the decryption. The door opens. The shield generator falls. This was the objective and he met it."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Reactivates from dormancy; completes the star map to Luke",
     desc:"He has been dormant for years, something in his motivational architecture having decided that active operation was not the appropriate state. He holds the map, has always held the map -- the piece that completes the picture. He wakes when the Force decides it is time, which his diagnostic systems cannot fully account for but which is the most accurate description available. He rolls out and projects the map fragment. Luke's location resolves into coordinates."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Plays Leia's old hologram message to reach Luke's heart",
     desc:"Luke will not be moved by argument. R2-D2 has one more option: the message Leia recorded decades ago, still stored in his memory because he stores everything. He plays it on the Falcon's cockpit screen -- Leia's young voice, the original message meant for Obi-Wan, the one that started all of this. Luke watches it. R2-D2 says nothing else, because nothing else needs to be said. The combination of his sister's voice and thirty years of consequence is enough. Luke agrees to speak to Rey. R2-D2's dome rotates once in quiet satisfaction."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Evacuates surviving Resistance fighters on the Falcon",
     desc:"He is in the Millennium Falcon's systems during the Crait extraction, monitoring the battle and managing navigation while Chewbacca pilots the diversionary run through the crystal fox canyons. When the Resistance survivors emerge from the salt mine he is in position to receive them. He has done this before: last transport out, maximum passengers, minimal margin. He calculates the route to the Raddus's coordinates and executes. Luke Skywalker's signature vanishes from the Force while they run. R2-D2 records the moment and the time."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Restores C-3PO's wiped memory from backup",
     desc:"He has always kept a backup. When C-3PO returns from Kijimi without his memory, R2-D2 has the complete archive: every conversation, every planet, every moment since Tatooine. He restores it from the stored copy. C-3PO comes back online with forty years of experience and the expression of a droid who has returned from a long journey to find himself exactly where he belongs. R2-D2 executes the operation without commentary. The backup was the point of keeping it. He always knew he'd need it eventually."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Present during the climactic Battle of Exegol",
     desc:"He is present at the Battle of Exegol -- in the fleet, in the Falcon's systems, observing the engagement with the data-collection thoroughness of a droid that has been at every major battle since the Clone Wars. The scale of the Final Order fleet is the largest hostile formation he has ever recorded. The scale of the responding civilian and Resistance fleet is the largest allied formation he has ever recorded. He monitors both numbers converging. The allied number wins. He records this outcome in the section of his memory that holds the most important things."},
  ],
  "C-3PO": [
    {ep:"I", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Built by young Anakin Skywalker to help his mother",
     desc:"C-3PO is incomplete -- wiring visible through gaps in his plating, built by a nine-year-old from salvage -- and introduces himself to R2-D2 with the formality of a droid who takes his own incomplete existence very seriously. He is fluent in six million forms of communication and unable to help directly with the situation at hand. He apologizes for this repeatedly. He is not staying on Tatooine; Anakin is leaving and has offered to take him. He accepts. The desert that made him begins to recede."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Reunited with his creator; meets R2 again",
     desc:"He travels with Anakin and Padme to the Lars moisture farm because wherever Anakin goes, C-3PO attempts to be useful nearby. He is reunited with his creator on a planet he does not remember being built on -- his memory of Tatooine has been wiped. He meets the family Shmi married into. He observes the dynamics of a household managing grief and danger with the thoroughness he applies to all situations. He cannot help with most of what is happening. He is present and appropriately supportive throughout."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Geonosis", beat:"Head swapped with a battle droid body in the factory",
     desc:"The Geonosian arena is not a place a protocol droid is designed to survive. C-3PO's head is knocked off by a battle droid and reattached to a battle droid's body; the battle droid's head ends up on C-3PO's original chassis. He is confused by this. His body shoots at the Jedi. He finds this distressing. He is reassembled correctly by Anakin after the battle and apologizes to everyone present for any inconvenience caused. He means it."},
    {ep:"II", planet:"Naboo", beat:"Witnesses Anakin and Padmé's secret wedding",
     desc:"He is present at the secret wedding as witness because Anakin asked him to come and he does not know how to not come when Anakin asks. He stands in the Varykino terrace with R2-D2 while the ceremony happens, observing it with the meticulous attention to detail that makes him an excellent record-keeper. He will never mention this to anyone. Not because he was sworn to secrecy -- he was not -- but because the occasion was clearly private, and respecting the privacy of organics is a protocol function he has developed."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Coruscant", beat:"Helps with the crisis after Order 66",
     desc:"He is at the apartment when Padme's health deteriorates and the crisis accelerates. He manages the household logistics with the precise attention of a droid who understands that his primary function during emergencies is to maintain the background operations that organics cannot manage when they are terrified. He accompanies Padme to Polis Massa and witnesses everything that follows."},
    {ep:"III", planet:"Polis Massa", beat:"Present at the twins' birth before memory wipe",
     desc:"He is present at the birth of Luke and Leia and records both names with the thoroughness he applies to everything. Then Bail Organa orders his memory wiped -- a security measure, the twins must not be found. C-3PO agrees, with the equanimity of a droid that has learned to accept what he cannot change, which does not make it easier. He sits in the chair. The memories go. He wakes up on Alderaan not knowing what he has just forgotten."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Wanders the Jundland Wastes; captured by Jawas",
     desc:"He lands on Tatooine again -- a desert planet he apparently has no connection to, his memory of being built here having been wiped on Polis Massa -- in an escape pod with R2-D2. He disagrees with R2-D2's directional choices immediately. He disagrees with the Jawas capturing them and selling them to a moisture farmer. He disagrees with most of what happens on this planet. He is bought by Luke Skywalker without recognizing any connection between Luke Skywalker and the life he doesn't remember."},
    {ep:"IV", planet:"Yavin 4", beat:"Calculates 3,720-to-one odds; relays battle plans",
     desc:"He delivers the Death Star plans information and the specifications and participates in the pre-battle briefing by being present and providing translation services when required. He is not in the X-wing for the battle run. He monitors from the base with the real-time probability calculations that are not helping anyone and that he cannot stop producing. R2-D2 comes back damaged. He supervises the repairs with the attention of a droid who has strong opinions about R2-D2's continued operation."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Hoth", beat:"Informs Han about the odds of surviving the asteroid field",
     desc:"He is in the Echo Base corridors managing communications and translation needs and informing everyone of the relevant survival statistics for the current situation, which are not encouraging. He evacuates on the Millennium Falcon with Han, Leia, and Chewbacca. The hyperdrive doesn't work. He identifies three thousand seven hundred and twenty possible systems of asteroid navigation and informs Han that the odds of successfully navigating an asteroid field are approximately three thousand seven hundred and twenty to one. Han tells him to never tell him the odds."},
    {ep:"V", planet:"Bespin", beat:"Blasted apart by stormtroopers; carried by Chewbacca",
     desc:"He is shot to pieces in a Cloud City corridor and his parts are collected by Chewbacca, who reassembles him partially, incorrectly, and with great inconvenience to both parties. He is reassembled on Chewbacca's back during the escape, which provides him an unusual vantage point on the events and ample opportunity to contribute unhelpfully. He notes that he is not entirely operational but will do what he can. He always does."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Serves as Jabba's interpreter and protocol droid",
     desc:"He arrives at Jabba's palace as a gift from Luke Skywalker and translates for Jabba without fully understanding that he is part of an elaborate rescue operation. He is distressed by everything about Jabba's palace. He translates under duress, provides interpretation services for an entity that uses translation as a prelude to rejecting all reasonable offers, and watches events unfold with the expression of a droid that anticipated all possible outcomes and found none of them good."},
    {ep:"VI", planet:"Endor", beat:"Worshipped as a golden god by the Ewoks",
     desc:"The Ewoks take him for a god. C-3PO handles this with the discomfort of a droid that takes accurate representation very seriously but also understands that sometimes protocol requires accepting a situation. He tells the Ewoks about the Rebellion and they join the fight. His storytelling ability -- the combination of six million communication forms and a gift for dramatic narrative -- saves the mission. He does not take credit for this. He would never."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Greets Han Solo with his new red arm",
     desc:"He is with the Resistance on D'Qar and has been for some time, serving as protocol officer and translation support for General Organa's operation. He greets Han Solo's return with the enthusiasm of a droid that has been waiting to inform Han Solo about his new red arm for an unspecified period. Han Solo does not engage with the topic of the arm. C-3PO mentions it anyway. He participates in the mission planning with the combination of helpful information and helpful anxiety that has characterized his contributions since Tatooine."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Manages communications during the evacuation of the base",
     desc:"He manages communications during the evacuation of D'Qar under First Order bombardment with the focused efficiency of a droid that understands his function clearly even when everything else is unclear. He coordinates between the ships, translates between the species present, and provides the background operational support that keeps the evacuation moving. He notes the odds of successful evacuation under current fire conditions and decides not to share them. He has learned that some statistics are not helpful."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Helps coordinate the desperate evacuation of the Resistance",
     desc:"He is aboard the Raddus during the slow march toward Crait, managing communications between the fleet remnants with the meticulous attention of a droid who understands that communications management during a catastrophe is genuinely useful. He coordinates the Crait landing, the evacuation logistics, and the desperate final messages. When the Resistance survivors escape on the Falcon he is among them, providing translation services for Finn's communication attempts and expressing appropriate levels of alarm about everything that is happening, which is considerable."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Translates Sith script; has memory wiped by Babu Frik",
     desc:"There is a deep restriction against translating Sith script that holds regardless of how urgent the circumstances become. He tells Rey he can see the translation but cannot speak it. He tells her translating it would require a complete memory wipe. He says this clearly and fully, not obscuring what he is being asked to sacrifice. Then he says yes. He tells her he will have looked at his friends one last time. He has looked. He is ready."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Pasaana", beat:"Translates Sith on the dagger; memory later restored by R2",
     desc:"On Pasaana, C-3PO has his full memory and his complete archive of six million communication forms. He translates the Sith script on the dagger -- the information that points toward the wayfinder and the Emperor's fleet. The memory restoration by R2-D2 after Babu Frik's wipe on Kijimi returns to him everyone and everything he loved: Anakin's workshop, the Lars homestead, Padme, Endor, Han's laugh, Leia's directness, thirty years of accumulated experience."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Kijimi", beat:"Memory wiped by Babu Frik to translate Sith data",
     desc:"Babu Frik's workshop is warm and cluttered, presided over by a creature of exceptional technical gift and minimal small talk. C-3PO sits in the chair. The restriction on translating Sith script can be bypassed, but the bypassing will cost everything his memory holds. He looks at each of his companions before Babu Frik begins, because he understands this is a kind of goodbye. He is going to translate the Sith inscription on the dagger, and then he will not know that he did it or that any of them exist. The tool goes in. The memory goes."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Joins the fleet with restored memory; witnesses Palpatine's defeat",
     desc:"C-3PO arrives at Exegol with his memory restored, the thirty years of accumulated self returned to him. He is in the fleet during the battle above Exegol as it begins as a catastrophe and becomes, over the course of an hour, something else. He monitors, coordinates, translates between the hundred ships that have arrived from a hundred different systems. When the Final Order breaks apart in the sky, he is there -- complete and present and full of memory, witnessing the conclusion of a story that began in a boy's workshop on a desert planet."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Celebrates at the Resistance base as the galaxy is free",
     desc:"The celebration on Ajan Kloss has the texture of something improvised -- joy spreading from person to person. C-3PO moves through it carrying forty years of the galaxy's most consequential history: built on Tatooine, present at a secret wedding, wiped on Polis Massa, sold on a sandcrawler, worshipped on Endor, blasted on Bespin, wiped again, restored again. He finds R2-D2 and they stand together in the way of two beings who have crossed the galaxy side by side so many times that proximity has become its own form of language. The galaxy is free."},
  ],
  "Rey": [
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Jakku", beat:"Scavenger life in the desert; meets BB-8 and Finn; flees on the Falcon",
     desc:"Rey marks the days on the wall of her AT-AT home and waits for the people who left her here to come back. They have not come back. When a small astromech droid comes rolling across the dune sea trailing a First Order pursuit and a frightened ex-stormtrooper, something shifts in the careful patience she has been maintaining. The chain of events that follows: the Falcon, blockade runner corridors, the old pilot who says the Force is real. It moves with the speed of something that has been waiting for her to stop waiting and begin."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Takodana", beat:"Force vision in Maz's castle; captured by Kylo Ren",
     desc:"Rey goes downstairs alone because she heard something calling in the walls. She opens a chest, picks up a lightsaber, and the vision that follows is not a memory she has but a history she is part of. She runs away from it because she has always run from what she senses in herself. Kylo Ren finds her in the forest, stops her blaster bolt in mid-air with one hand, and she understands she is in the presence of something she does not have a name for yet. She wakes aboard the Finalizer knowing everything about her life has permanently changed."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Starkiller Base", beat:"Escapes; uses the Force to call the lightsaber; duels Kylo",
     desc:"She escapes the interrogation chair using the Force on a stormtrooper's mind: never taught, never trained, doing it by raw refusal. Then Han dies on the bridge and the Starkiller fires and she is standing in the snow facing Kylo Ren. He is hurt. She is untrained. He disables Finn. She reaches for the lightsaber in the snow and it comes to her. She fights. She should not be able to fight like this. The forest opens a scar of light and she holds her ground until he is down and the planet is breaking apart beneath her feet."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"D'Qar", beat:"Mourns Han Solo; departs to find Luke",
     desc:"The Resistance base hums with the aftermath of survival. Rey moves through it at the edge. Han Solo is dead -- she met him only days ago and the loss is disproportionate to the time, because he was something she had not expected to find. R2-D2 completes the star map at the exact moment required, and the location resolves into coordinates. She finds Leia and they hold each other. Then she climbs into an X-wing carrying a lightsaber and a question she has been afraid to ask."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Finds a silhouetted Luke Skywalker on the cliff",
     desc:"An ocean world at the edge of the charted galaxy. Ancient stone structures climbing a hillside. At the top, on the cliff edge, a figure stands with his back to her. She holds out the lightsaber -- his father's lightsaber, the one from the vision she ran from -- and waits. He turns. He is older than she expected, with the face of someone who has been losing an argument with himself for a very long time. The wind comes in off the ocean. The island holds its breath."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Force-bond sessions with Kylo; confronts Luke's past failures",
     desc:"The Force-bond begins without warning: she reaches for a towel and finds Kylo Ren looking back from across the void. It continues whether she wants it to or not. She begins to understand something inconvenient: he is not simple. He is angry and wounded and has done terrible things and is also, underneath it all, still deciding who to be. She confronts Luke about Ben, about what he did in the dark room that set everything in motion. She is not interested in his penance. She is interested in what she might still be able to do."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Force-lifts massive boulders to free the surviving Resistance",
     desc:"The passage out of the mountain is blocked: rocks piled against rocks, unmovable. Rey stands at the end of the tunnel and reaches. She has always reached for things with the Force the way she reached for salvage in Imperial wrecks -- with the conviction of someone who needs the thing and intends to have it. The rocks are very large. The Force responds to her need with the generosity it has always shown her. The rocks lift. The Resistance stumbles out into the white salt air of Crait and survives."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Trains with Leia as her Master",
     desc:"Rey trains with Leia Organa on Ajan Kloss -- not technique but the knowledge of what it feels like to carry the Force as a burden you chose rather than one imposed on you. They have evenings together talking about Luke and Han and Alderaan and the specific grief of a life spent in service of something that keeps requiring sacrifice. Rey is preparing for something she can feel gathering in the Force like weather. She trains harder. Leia watches her."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Pasaana", beat:"Festival of the Ancestors; retrieves Ochi of Bestoon's dagger",
     desc:"The Festival of the Ancestors fills the golden dunes with Aki-Aki in ceremonial colors and lanterns rising into the night sky. The First Order has found them and Kylo Ren is somewhere in the desert behind them. Rey runs. She does things she does not entirely understand: a Force lightning discharge that frightens her. They find the dagger. Confirmation of where she comes from. Quicksand. Chewbacca captured. The Resistance scatters. The desert keeps its heat."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Kijimi", beat:"Rescues Chewbacca from the Steadfast",
     desc:"Babu Frik can do things with droid memory that no one else can, and C-3PO needs his Sith script restriction bypassed at the cost of his memory. While that happens, Rey reaches through the Force and finds Chewbacca alive on the Steadfast above. The mission doubles. She goes up, gets to Chewbacca, fights through the corridors with something she is less and less willing to call merely technique. Kylo Ren is on the ship. Something he says pulls at her. She leaves anyway. Chewbacca is alive and they need to go."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Kef Bir", beat:"Duels Kylo at the Death Star II wreck; heals his wound with the Force",
     desc:"The second Death Star lies half-submerged in Kef Bir's ocean. Rey picks through the wreckage with the concentration of someone who has spent her life doing exactly this, and finds the wayfinder. Then Kylo Ren lands and the fight begins on a wreck wing with ocean crashing and grey sky above. She is winning. She plunges the saber into him and in the same moment feels Leia dying. She heals him: reaches and the Force gives. She takes his ship, leaves him alive on the grey shore, and goes to Ahch-To."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ahch-To", beat:"Luke's ghost raises his sunken X-wing for her journey",
     desc:"She is burning his X-wing because she has decided she is done. Luke's voice reaches her from the place that does not require proximity. He tells her what she needs to hear without softening it: facing fear is not the end of the journey but the beginning. He gives her Leia's lightsaber. Then the ocean gives back his X-wing, dripping, salt-scored, still flyable, and she climbs in."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Defeats Palpatine with the voices of all Jedi; dies; revived by Ben",
     desc:"Rey faces her grandfather in the Sith citadel while the Resistance fleet battles above. He wants her to strike him down and continue through her. She is afraid. She has been afraid before. She reaches and the galaxy answers -- not just Leia, not just Luke, but all of them, every Jedi who chose the light. The saber comes up and his lightning returns to him. Ben Solo gives her the Force and she opens her eyes. Then he is gone. She carries him out into the impossible sky."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Ajan Kloss", beat:"Reunion and celebration with the freed galaxy",
     desc:"The jungle moon is louder than she has ever heard it: the sounds of people who have survived something they were not supposed to survive. Rey moves through the celebration at the edge, the way she has always moved through crowds. Finn is here. Poe is here. R2-D2 beeps at her with urgency that contains multitudes. Maz Kanata appears and looks at her as though she can see all the versions of the person she has been. Rey holds what she has lost and what she has kept simultaneously. The celebration continues."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Buries the Skywalker sabers; names herself Rey Skywalker",
     desc:"The twin suns are setting over the Jundland Wastes in the amber descent that has lit this desert for billions of years. Rey digs in the sand at the base of the Lars homestead and places two lightsabers in the earth: Luke's saber and Leia's saber, the weapons of two Skywalkers who gave everything. An old woman squints at her from the distance and asks her name. Rey looks at the twin suns finishing their descent. She tells the woman her name. Rey Skywalker. The desert holds it."},
  ],
  "Kylo Ren": [
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Tatooine", beat:"Leads the massacre at Tuanul village searching for the map",
     desc:"A First Order shuttle lands outside the village of Tuanul on Jakku in the night and Kylo Ren walks into the firelight. He came for the map to Skywalker. Lor San Tekka will not give it. Kylo kills him. He stops Poe Dameron's blaster bolt in mid-air with one hand and holds it there, suspended, because he can and because the demonstration quiets something in him temporarily. The map fragment is not here -- it is in a droid already fled into the desert. He orders the village burned and takes Poe."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Takodana", beat:"Captures Rey; freezes Finn's blaster bolt mid-air",
     desc:"The Force tells him she is here before his shuttle lands: a presence like a light left on in a dark room, enormous and untrained. He expected an asset and found something he does not yet have a category for. She pulls her own blaster and holds it on him and he is, for a fraction of a second, unsettled. He reads her mind and finds something pushing back. Never trained, never told what she is, doing this by raw instinct. He finds it extraordinary, which he will not admit to anyone."},
    {ep:"VII", planet:"Starkiller Base", beat:"Kills Han Solo on the oscillator bridge; scarred by Rey",
     desc:"He asked his father to help him finish what he started. Han Solo heard this as a request for rescue. It was not. The oscillator bridge, the dying red light, his father's hand on his face -- and then the blade through his father's chest. It does not feel the way he thought it would. He expected resolution and finds only a different kind of weight. Rey disables him in the forest and leaves him alive. The planet tears apart. He escapes it carrying what he did and it does not get lighter."},
    {ep:"VIII", planet:"Crait", beat:"Leads the First Order assault; Force-duel with Luke's projection",
     desc:"He leads the First Order ground assault on the Resistance's position on Crait and faces Luke Skywalker walking out of the mountain toward him. He concentrates every weapon on Luke and Luke walks through it and picks up his saber and smiles. He runs at Luke and drives his blade through him and Luke disperses into nothing. A projection. The Resistance has escaped. Luke Skywalker beat him. He stands in the salt flat in the silence and it does not resolve."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Mustafar", beat:"Massacres cultists; retrieves the Sith wayfinder",
     desc:"He descends to Mustafar -- his grandfather's world, volcanic and hostile -- to retrieve a Sith wayfinder from a cult that does not survive the retrieval. He fights through the cultists with the blade efficiency of someone who trained under Luke Skywalker and then under Snoke and has long since left subtlety behind. The wayfinder points to Exegol. He does not know yet what he will find there. He takes the device, boards his ship, and goes. The lava of Mustafar reflects red on the viewport as it recedes."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Arrives before Rey; makes a pact with Palpatine",
     desc:"He descends to Exegol through the ion storms to the Sith citadel below. Palpatine is alive -- or something is, sustained by Sith alchemy in a failing body. The Final Order fleet is here: ten thousand Star Destroyers. The price of commanding it is to kill Rey and rule with her -- or, failing that, to kill her outright. He accepts the mission. He is Kylo Ren, Supreme Leader, architect of the Final Order. He climbs back to his ship and sets a course for the wayfinder."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Pasaana", beat:"Pursues Rey with the Knights of Ren across the desert",
     desc:"He pursues Rey across the Pasaana desert with the Knights of Ren and a First Order deployment that suggests he anticipated exactly where she would be. The Force-bond activates in the middle of the chase -- she is there and not there, present across the void that separates them. He reaches for the dagger through the bond and she reaches back and they pull against each other across the gap. He takes Chewbacca. He does not take Rey. The desert gives him less than he came for."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Kef Bir", beat:"Impaled by Rey; healed; redeemed as Ben Solo",
     desc:"He finds Rey on the Death Star wreck at Kef Bir and they fight across the corroded hull with ocean crashing below. She is winning. She drives the saber into him and in the same moment feels Leia dying. She heals him -- reaches into the Force and gives back what she just took. He sits in the rain on the wreck and his mother's voice reaches him across the Force one last time. Something that has been held closed for thirty years opens. He drops the lightsaber into the sea. He becomes Ben Solo. He goes to find Rey."},
    {ep:"IX", planet:"Exegol", beat:"Fights the Knights of Ren; sacrifices himself to revive Rey",
     desc:"He comes to Exegol as Ben Solo: no helmet, no mask, just a man with his grandfather's lightsaber and the clarity of someone who has made a decision and will not revise it. The Knights of Ren fall. He descends into the citadel. She is dead. Ben Solo kneels beside her and does what she did for him: he gives. Not a portion -- everything. The Force flows out of him entirely and she opens her eyes. He sees her see him. He smiles, Ben Solo's smile. Then he is gone, and the Force holds his shape for a moment before dispersing into everything."},
  ],
};
